<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/docs/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
import SendInvoice from './SendInvoice.abide'
import sendInvoiceSource from './SendInvoice.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <p><code>reachable</code> asks the host once and remembers the answer, so the thirty are refused
    at once.</p>

    <Section title="The form of `reachable`">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>What you get</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>await reachable('api.example.com')</code></td><td>whether the host answered, asked over https</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>await reachable('http://billing.internal:8080')</code></td><td>the same, of an origin asked as written</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>There is one form and no options. It is a server name, imported from
        <code>@abide/abide/server</code>, and its signature is in the
        <a href={url('/reference/helpers')}>Helpers</a> reference.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`reachable` goes ahead of the call">
        <Example
            id="reachable-host"
            title="`reachable(host)` — ask once, refuse at once"
            summary="Invoice 4310, and a billing service that has stopped answering. Send it twice — the first send waits out the check, the second is refused before it leaves."
            files={{
                'SendInvoice.abide': sendInvoiceSource,
            }}
        >
            <SendInvoice />
        </Example>

        <p>Ask, and refuse where the answer is no. The first send waits out the five-second probe
        (23.11); every send after it in the next minute is answered from what that probe learned and
        refused at once (23.12), which the message's own timing shows.</p>

        <p>The refusal is an ordinary one, so the page that called renders it the way it renders any
        other — see <a href={url('/data/refuse-a-request-and-say-why')}>Failures</a>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`reachable` holds each host's answer for a minute">
        <p>The first ask of a host sends one <code>HEAD</code> to it. Every ask of that host in the
        60 seconds after it is answered from what the first one learned, by every caller in the
        process.</p>

        <p>That is what the name is for. A host that is down costs one probe a minute instead of one
        timeout per request, and a host that is up costs one <code>HEAD</code> a minute on top of
        the calls themselves. Asks that arrive while the probe is in flight share it rather than
        sending more.</p>

        <p>So the answer can be a minute old in either direction. A host that came back is refused
        for the rest of its minute, and a host that just went down is called once more by each
        request inside it — the call's own failure is still yours to handle.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Any answer from the host counts as reachable">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>The probe</th><th><code>reachable</code></th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>answered <code>200</code>, <code>404</code> or <code>500</code></td><td><code>true</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>was refused, or the name did not resolve</td><td><code>false</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>went 5 seconds unanswered</td><td><code>false</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>The question is whether something is there to call, and a <code>404</code> for
        <code>/</code> came from something. Whether the host is healthy is a different question, and
        the call that follows answers it.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/data/refuse-a-request-and-say-why')}>Failures</a> — what a caller
            gets when the answer is no</li>
            <li><a href={url('/data/put-a-ceiling-on-a-request')}>Limits</a> — a ceiling on the
            call that does go ahead</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
